# ===========================================================================
# SET-KIND DERIVATION TABLE (A3, 2026-08-25).
#
# Mirrors the canonical pack module
#   dogany-agentpack-health-trainer/payload/service/health/session_harness/
#   set_kind.py
# ROW FOR ROW, so the later harness landing is a re-wire and not a rewrite.
# The canonical file is NOT edited from here; this is the live-instance twin
# that runs today, on today's schema.
#
#   set_kind | in_table | keyboard        | volume | cue shape
#   ---------|----------|-----------------|--------|-------------------------
#   prep     | yes      | completion tap  | no     | exercise name + target
#   ramp     | yes      | reps drilldown  | no     | load + target
#   working  | yes      | reps drilldown  | yes    | load + target + RIR
#   hold     | yes      | timer (done)    | yes    | duration
#
# WHY A TABLE AND NOT BRANCHES -- measured, session 10092 (2026-08-25):
#   D1  prep drills lived OUTSIDE the set pipeline entirely: zero DB rows, zero
#       table rows, no prescription, rendered as a single-option `1. 완료` menu
#       (a zero-decision round trip). Owner, 06:28 and again 07:06.
#   D2  ramp turns emitted PROSE ONLY -- no table, no cue, no keyboard -- on
#       barbell_row / leg_curl / bicep_curl / standing_calf_raise, while
#       set-add's own stdout for the same rows carried [[IDRILL:...]] and a
#       SET_CUE: line. Ramp rows were inside the DB pipeline and outside the
#       BLOCK pipeline.
# With a total table there is no outside: every unit of work is a row, and what
# differs between the kinds is READ from here, never re-decided at the call
# site.
#
# THE PREP CELL IS A COMPLETION TAP, NOT A REP KEYBOARD (owner ruling
# 2026-08-27).
#
# Reps exist to MEASURE VOLUME. A working set is a CHALLENGE set: its outcome
# genuinely varies, so the count has to be measured. Prep is prescribed on the
# assumption that it is completed, so asking the count asks something already
# known -- and if the drill CANNOT be completed that is a PRESCRIPTION DEFECT,
# not data. Logging a lower number launders the defect into the volume trend.
#
# There is a second, harder reason. `target_reps` is ONE INTEGER COLUMN that
# carries FOUR INCOMPATIBLE UNITS across a real prep plan --
#     total reps       a plain repetition count
#     per-side reps    a count PER SIDE; the true total is DOUBLE
#     rounds           rounds of a multi-exercise circuit
#     hold sets        a number of timed hold SETS (seconds live in hold_sec)
# -- and some drills carry no value at all. A rep keyboard coerces all of that
# into one integer, and the coercion is not hypothetical: a one-ROUND circuit
# was stored as `reps=1`, i.e. one repetition. The unit is now DECLARED
# (prep.yaml `target_unit`, read here by prep_target_spec) and travels with the
# value instead of being lost in a column named REPS.
#
# The `hold` row is untouched and its `timer_done` token is still derivable from
# `hold` ALONE: a prep drill takes one tap because its outcome is prescribed, a
# hold takes a timer because its outcome is a duration. Two different reasons,
# two different tokens.
#
# THE `set_kind` COLUMN. Migration 034_set_kind (set_kind / target_reps /
# hold_sec) adds it; a DB that predates the migration simply does not have it,
# and set-add PROBES rather than assumes (its _workout_sets_columns).
#
# The defect that made the write matter: while the column existed but nothing
# WROTE it, the schema's `NOT NULL DEFAULT 'working'` quietly stamped `working`
# onto prep rows. A backfill at migration time hid it, because only rows written
# AFTERWARDS took the default.
#
# The SIGN CONVENTION below is still the DERIVER of record, because it answers
# from the row alone and needs no column read; the column is what set-add now
# WRITES (see its --set-kind / --target-reps / --hold-sec), so the stored row and
# the derived kind agree instead of one contradicting the other.
# ===========================================================================
