# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RUNG CEILING (2026-09-11, incident A). ENFORCEMENT for a code that had
# only ever been a NAME.
#
# MEASURED, session 10103, standing_calf_raise:
#     set_seq -1   115.0kg  20 reps      <- the warm-up
#     set_seq  1   115.0kg  20 reps      <- the working set
# The owner, in the gym: 「115로 증량갑니다하고 웜업을 115로시키네」.
#
# RAMP_R_NO_RUNG ("no_rung_below_working") has existed since A3 and states this
# exact rule, but nothing ever CHECKED it -- the string named a condition and
# no branch asserted it. _snap_rungs filters `w >= working_weight` against the
# argument IT was handed, which is a different question from "below the load the
# owner is actually about to lift": on the arm path that argument is
# ramp_basis()'s weight (an override / the slot / last session's log), and on
# the record path (set-add --weight) there is no argument at all.
#
# So the ceiling moves to the PUBLICATION EXIT and takes the weight it is
# measured against as an explicit parameter. Every path that mints, re-plans or
# records a rung passes its OWN ceiling through this one function, and a rung
# that reaches or passes it is not a warm-up -- it is the working set wearing
# another name (the rule _stack_setting_weight already states for the 세팅 rung,
# generalised to the whole ladder).
#
# REFUSE, DO NOT PATCH. A ladder that cannot be built below the working load
# yields NO ladder and the reason code that says so, because the alternative --
# quietly shipping the rungs that do fit -- is how "we could not work out a
# warm-up" becomes "no warm-up is needed" (ramp_lane_open's own doctrine, one
# scope narrower). Note that dropping an over-ceiling rung and KEEPING the
# survivors is still correct and still happens: it is only the empty result that
# becomes a refusal, and the refusal names itself.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def gate_rung_ceiling(rows, working_weight):
    """Enforce `rung weight < working weight` over an already-planned ladder.

    Returns (rows, reason):
      * (rows, None)              every rung sits below the ceiling
      * (kept, None)              some rung did not; the survivors stand
      * ([], RAMP_R_NO_RUNG)      nothing survives -> publish no ramp at all

    `working_weight` None (calibration / unresolved) -> (rows, None) unchanged:
    an unknown ceiling is not a ceiling of zero, and refusing a ladder over our
    own missing number is the fail-open this file already rules for elsewhere.
    A rung with `weight_kg` None (the genuine bodyweight primer) is never
    measured -- it has no load to compare.

    Pure: no I/O, no clock, no DB. The one place the invariant is stated as an
    executable condition rather than as a constant's name."""
    if working_weight is None:
        return list(rows or []), None
    try:
        ceiling = float(working_weight)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return list(rows or []), None
    kept = []
    for r in (rows or []):
        w = r.get("weight_kg")
        if w is None:
            kept.append(r)
            continue
        try:
            if float(w) < ceiling - 1e-9:
                kept.append(r)
        except (TypeError, ValueError):
            kept.append(r)     # unreadable weight is not a ceiling breach
    if not kept:
        return [], RAMP_R_NO_RUNG
    return kept, None


def _stack_setting_weight(working_weight, grid):
    """The 세팅 rung's weight for a PIN-STACK lift, or None when this lift is
    not a stack, nothing declares its grid, or no settable value sits below the
    working load.

    OWNER RULING 2026-08-25 12:07 -- this REVERSES the 2026-08-14 rule
    "스택류(케이블·핀) -> 프라이머 생략". Owner ruling: a plate-loaded machine
    also has an empty-carriage
    거나, 스택 머신도 낮은 무게로 세팅 잡는 거 가능한데." Asked whether to apply
    it to all three equipment classes or leave stacks optional, they said "전부".

    MEASURED TRIGGER, 2026-08-25 07:07. lat_pulldown is a stack, had no 세팅
    rung, and the user objected that no warm-up was offered before a heavy
    first set. The
    engine had no way to give them one because the rule EXCLUDED stacks.
    face_pull (cable) had the same hole. One name, three forms -- the equipment
    decides the trailing description, not the label:
        바벨·스미스        빈 봉 (20kg)
        플레이트로디드     원판 없이 (무부하)
        스택(케이블·핀)    낮은 무게 한 세트      <- this function

    WHERE THE NUMBER COMES FROM -- read off the existing ladder, not invented,
    and no new constant:
      * the light end of this ladder is ALREADY declared as
        _MACHINE_RAMP_FLOOR_FRAC. _machine_ramp_rungs computes
        floor = working * that fraction and emits rungs STRICTLY ABOVE it, so
        the value AT the floor is the one weight the ladder already computes and
        deliberately drops. That is exactly the slot a 세팅 set occupies: one
        step below the ramp, light enough to be a setup rather than a lift.
      * _machine_ramp_rungs' OWN fallback ("no clean unit yielded a ladder ->
        a single light rung at the snapped floor") is already this behaviour.
        This promotes it to a named rung instead of leaving it a degrade path.
      * it is then snapped through the DECLARED grid with the SAME rounder every
        other rung and the working weight use (ax_engine.snap_to_lattice). A
        stack's increments are fixed by the machine, so the snap is what makes
        this a real pin position instead of a plausible number.

    GATED ON A DECLARED STACK GRID (`type == 'stack'`), which is precisely the
    pin-stack class and nothing else: ax_machine loading_method 'stack' (cable
    stations and pin machines) maps to it, 'plate_loaded' maps to 'standard' and
    a fixed dumbbell to 'even' (_MACHINE_LOADING_TO_GRID_TYPE). So the BARBELL
    path and the PLATE-LOADED path are byte-identical to before this ruling.
    An undeclared grid yields None -- an unmeasured stack is a data gap to fill,
    never a number to guess (the same rule _snap_rungs already states)."""
    if working_weight is None or not isinstance(grid, dict):
        return None
    if str(grid.get("type", "")).strip().lower() != "stack":
        return None
    try:
        working = float(working_weight)
        w = ax_engine().snap_to_lattice(working * _MACHINE_RAMP_FLOOR_FRAC,
                                        grid)
    except Exception:      # noqa: BLE001 -- a snap miss must never break a table
        return None
    if w is None:
        return None
    w = round(float(w), 4)
    # A 세팅 that is not BELOW the working load is not a 세팅 -- it would be the
    # working set wearing another name. Better no rung than a false one.
    if w <= 0 or w >= working - 1e-9:
        return None
    return w


def _e1rm_rungs(e1rm, barbell):
    """Warm-up rung loads as % of e1RM (e1rm_rx_rules.json ramp.pct_of_e1rm),
    ascending and unsnapped. A barbell rung is first rounded to the rules'
    barbell_step_kg above the empty bar (no micro plates) -- the declared grid
    snap in _snap_rungs then runs as for any rung."""
    rules = _E1.load_rules()
    loads = _E1.ramp_loads(e1rm, rules)
    if barbell:
        step = float(rules["ramp"]["barbell_step_kg"])
        loads = [_RAMP_EMPTY_BAR_KG
                 + round((w - _RAMP_EMPTY_BAR_KG) / step) * step
                 for w in loads]
    return loads


def _cap_e1rm_rungs(rungs, working_weight):
    """Drop SNAPPED e1RM rungs above the rules' ramp.max_frac_of_working of
    today's working load: that close to the working set a rung is a second
    working set, not a warm-up. Measured after the grid snap, because the snap
    can lift a rung over the cap (lat_pulldown 66.97 -> 70 under a 75 load)."""
    cap = _E1.load_rules()["ramp"].get("max_frac_of_working")
    if cap is None or not working_weight:
        return rungs
    return [w for w in rungs if w <= float(working_weight) * float(cap) + 1e-9]


def ramp_e1rm_for(db_path, exercise_name, slot, session_id=None):
    """The e1RM this exercise's warm-up is cut from today, or None (the
    pre-ruling ladder). The SAME eligibility + sufficiency gate rx-calc uses
    for the working load, with the same exclusion of this open session and the
    same machine scope (kit 062), so the ladder switches exactly when the
    working prescription does. Never raises."""
    if _E1 is None:
        return None
    try:
        return _E1.ramp_e1rm(db_path, exercise_name, slot,
                             exclude_session_id=session_id,
                             machine=_confirmed_machine(db_path, session_id,
                                                        exercise_name))
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 -- a ladder never breaks on the estimate
        return None


def _confirmed_machine(db_path, session_id, exercise_name):
    """The lift's confirmed machine (kit 062; machine_capture), or None --
    the same scope rx-calc's working load reads, so the ladder and the
    working set come from one machine's history."""
    try:
        import machine_capture as _MC
        return _MC.confirmed_machine(db_path, session_id, exercise_name)
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        return None


# -- the re-measure check set (owner ruling 2026-09-30 13:28) ----------------
# The first session a lift is prescribed on the e1RM basis, rx-calc marks
# rx_basis.measure=true: the FIRST working set is a check set at the prescribed
# load, open reps, stopping with rx_basis.measure_rir reps left. Its e1RM
# becomes the lift's anchor (e1rm_rx.find_anchor). The card says so in one
# line, only while that set is still ahead (no working set logged yet).
# set-table's owner-facing lines are literals in this file (no copy catalog
# covers them); this is the one place the wording lives.
MEASURE_CUE_KO = ("[확인 세트] 1세트는 횟수 상관없이 %s개 남을 때까지 해요. "
                  "이 기록으로 무게를 다시 잡아요.")


def measure_cue_line(rx_basis, work):
    """The check-set cue line for the card, or None. Shown only when rx-calc
    flagged today's first working set as the measurement (rx_basis.measure) and
    no working set of this exercise is logged yet in the session."""
    rb = rx_basis or {}
    if not rb.get("measure") or work:
        return None
    rir = rb.get("measure_rir") or [1, 2]
    try:
        lo, hi = int(rir[0]), int(rir[-1])
    except (TypeError, ValueError, IndexError):
        lo, hi = 1, 2
    return MEASURE_CUE_KO % ("%d~%d" % (lo, hi) if lo != hi else "%d" % lo)


def ramp_decision(db_path, exercise_name, rx_weight, slot=None, e1rm=None):
    """The ramp decision for this exercise, AS A VALUE.

    Returns {"decision": RAMP_YES|RAMP_NO, "reason": <code>, "rows": [...]}.
    `rows` is the planned ladder (identical to planned_ramp_rows' return) and is
    [] whenever decision is RAMP_NO.

    `e1rm` (owner ruling 2026-09-30): the exercise's SUFFICIENT e1RM estimate
    (e1rm_rx.ramp_e1rm), or None. When given, the rungs are % of e1RM instead
    of fractions of the working load -- the working load may itself have been
    set low, and a warm-up cut from it is then doubly low. Every gate below
    (class, assist, empty bar, grid snap, the rung ceiling) is unchanged, and
    the YES result carries "basis": "e1rm". None -> byte-identical to before.

    There is no path out of this function that does not name a reason. That is
    the point: D3 was not a wrong decision, it was an UNINSPECTABLE one."""
    if rx_weight is None:
        return {"decision": RAMP_NO, "reason": RAMP_R_NO_WEIGHT, "rows": []}
    barbell = _is_ramp_exercise(db_path, exercise_name)
    machine = _is_machine_ramp_exercise(db_path, exercise_name)
    if not (barbell or machine):
        return {"decision": RAMP_NO, "reason": RAMP_R_NOT_RAMP_CLASS,
                "rows": []}
    if _is_assist_loaded(db_path, exercise_name):
        return {"decision": RAMP_NO, "reason": RAMP_R_ASSIST, "rows": []}
    grid = declared_load_grid(db_path, exercise_name, slot)
    if _has_unloaded_start(db_path, exercise_name, slot, grid):
        if rx_weight <= _RAMP_EMPTY_BAR_KG:
            return {"decision": RAMP_NO, "reason": RAMP_R_AT_OR_BELOW_BAR,
                    "rows": []}
        if e1rm and _E1 is not None:
            intermediates = _e1rm_rungs(e1rm, barbell=True)
        else:
            _step, intermediates = _ramp_pick_step(rx_weight)
        rows = [{"weight_kg": _RAMP_EMPTY_BAR_KG, "label": "bw",
                 "plate_note": ""}]
        snapped = _snap_rungs(intermediates, grid, rx_weight,
                              _RAMP_EMPTY_BAR_KG)
        if e1rm and _E1 is not None:
            snapped = _cap_e1rm_rungs(snapped, rx_weight)
        for idx, w in enumerate(snapped, start=1):
            rows.append({"weight_kg": w, "label": "r%d" % idx,
                         "plate_note": _plate_breakdown(w)})
        rows, ceil_r = gate_rung_ceiling(rows, rx_weight)
        if ceil_r is not None:
            return {"decision": RAMP_NO, "reason": ceil_r, "rows": []}
        out = {"decision": RAMP_YES, "reason": RAMP_R_BARBELL,
               "rows": _with_plate_deltas(rows)}
        if e1rm and _E1 is not None:
            out["basis"] = "e1rm"
        return out
    # 2026-08-25 12:07 ruling: a PIN STACK gets a 세팅 rung too. It sits at the
    # ladder's own floor (see _stack_setting_weight), so the ramp rungs are then
    # filtered from THAT floor rather than from 0 -- otherwise the first rung
    # snaps onto the same pin position and the owner is shown one weight twice.
    setting = _stack_setting_weight(rx_weight, grid)
    floor = 0.0 if setting is None else setting
    if e1rm and _E1 is not None:
        rungs = _cap_e1rm_rungs(
            _snap_rungs(_e1rm_rungs(e1rm, barbell=False), grid, rx_weight,
                        floor), rx_weight)
    else:
        rungs = _snap_rungs(_machine_ramp_rungs(rx_weight), grid, rx_weight,
                            floor)
    if not rungs and setting is None:
        return {"decision": RAMP_NO, "reason": RAMP_R_NO_RUNG, "rows": []}
    rows = []
    if setting is not None:
        # label stays `bw` -- the internal rung name is unchanged, so set-arm's
        # rung_label_for_seq/seq_for_rung_label round-trip (set_seq 0) and the
        # taper's empty-bar band keep working. Only the owner-facing SET column
        # renders it 세팅 (set_col_label).
        rows.append({"weight_kg": setting, "label": "bw", "plate_note": ""})
    rows.extend({"weight_kg": w, "label": "r%d" % idx, "plate_note": ""}
                for idx, w in enumerate(rungs, start=1))
    rows, ceil_r = gate_rung_ceiling(rows, rx_weight)
    if ceil_r is not None:
        return {"decision": RAMP_NO, "reason": ceil_r, "rows": []}
    out = {"decision": RAMP_YES, "reason": RAMP_R_MACHINE,
           "rows": _with_plate_deltas(rows)}
    if e1rm and _E1 is not None:
        out["basis"] = "e1rm"
    return out


def _with_plate_deltas(rows):
    """Stamp each rung with `plate_delta` -- the PER-SIDE kg to add relative to
    the rung before it (None on the first rung, which has nothing to diff
    against and is therefore the one absolute loading of the exercise).

    D6, measured: the only [원판] lines the owner saw on 2026-08-25 were
    `바 20kg + 양쪽 60kg` (06:52) and `바 20kg + 양쪽 25kg` (07:29) -- both
    ABSOLUTE, on the first WORKING set of a barbell lift. They had just ramped
    20 -> 60 -> 100 on that bar. They wants what to ADD, and the delta rule is
    already written down (SKILL G10) and already implemented (set-arm
    plate_cue_line). What was missing is that the RAMP RUNGS never entered the
    ledger the delta diffs against, so the first working set always degraded to
    the absolute form. The ladder is computed HERE, so the per-rung delta is
    stamped HERE, and set-arm's cue can read a rung's delta instead of
    re-deriving one. Non-plate ladders (machine/stack/cable) carry
    plate_delta=None throughout: no plates exist to move.

    Per-side, in kg, never in plate COUNTS -- decomposing a delta into the
    gym's actual inventory is a guess, and a wrong count is worse than an
    honest kg (SKILL G10)."""
    prev = None
    for r in rows:
        w = r.get("weight_kg")
        if not r.get("plate_note") or prev is None or w is None:
            r["plate_delta"] = None
        else:
            r["plate_delta"] = round((float(w) - float(prev)) / 2.0, 4)
        prev = w
    return rows


def planned_ramp_rows(db_path, exercise_name, rx_weight, slot=None):
    """Compute the planned warmup+ramp row list for this exercise and working
    weight. Returns a list of dicts [{weight_kg, label, plate_note}, ...] in
    ascending order.

    A3 (2026-08-25): this is now a THIN VIEW over ramp_decision(), which is the
    SoT. The list return is preserved byte-for-byte for the existing callers
    (set-arm's rung arm, session-cursor, the loading suites); a caller that
    needs to know WHY the list is empty calls ramp_decision() instead. There is
    exactly one planner, so the two answers can never disagree.

    WHETHER a ramp is included is unchanged (F6 + F6-ext, owner-confirmed
    2026-08-19): compound+freeweight lifts and machine/stack lifts qualify,
    everything else returns []. That decision is deterministic -- never a model
    omission guess.

    WHICH ramp it is now follows the DECLARED data, not a hand-written equipment
    branch (2026-08-24, the incline-dumbbell-press incident):
      * an UNLOADED START STATE exists (barbell/smith: the empty 20kg bar) ->
        empty bar first, then bar+plate intermediates; plate_note (#39) = the
        per-side breakdown for each rung.
      * it does NOT (fixed dumbbell, machine, stack) -> NO empty-bar rung, just
        a short light ladder stepping up to the working weight in that
        equipment's own load unit (_machine_ramp_rungs). plate_note is "" for
        every rung, so the #39 plate cue stays barbell-only.
    Either way EVERY rung is gated through the exercise's declared load grid
    (_snap_rungs), so a ramp weight is always a value the equipment can be set
    to -- the 25.0kg-on-an-even-kg-dumbbell-rack defect.

    Empty list only when the exercise does not qualify, the weight is unknown,
    or the ladder has no rung below the working weight."""
    return ramp_decision(db_path, exercise_name, rx_weight, slot=slot)["rows"]


