def _fmt_vol(v):
    """Volume kg for the header: verbatim like weights (drop a trailing .0 only
    when whole, else keep the decimal so 637.5 stays 637.5)."""
    if float(v).is_integer():
        return format(int(v), ",")
    return format(float(v), ",.2f").rstrip("0").rstrip(".")



# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OWNER SURFACE vs MACHINE RAIL -- and why the surface is DECLARED, not sniffed
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES (measured 2026-08-30/31, live session 10094). main()
# prints the fenced table and then its own machine signal lines to the SAME
# stdout. Two live rails post that stdout to the owner's chat VERBATIM with no
# model turn in between -- the bridge's idrill `followup_cmd` reply and the
# fastpath handler body -- so the owner read
#     DAY_SOURCE exercise=smith_seated_calf_raise day=... source=session_type
#     RAMP_DECISION exercise=... decision=ramp reason=machine_ladder rungs=2
# on the owner's phone, once per working set. SKILL.md's "machine markers never reach
# the owner" is a rule the MODEL executes; on a zero-model-turn rail there is
# no model, so the rule never runs. A missing gate, not a prompt violation.
#
# THE FIRST ATTEMPT AT THIS GATE FAILED IN PRODUCTION, AND HOW IT FAILED IS THE
# REASON FOR THE SHAPE BELOW. It resolved the surface from CONTEXT: a
# model-runtime env marker meant "machine", a bridge process ancestor meant
# "owner", nothing proved meant "owner". It measured 54/54 in a detached test
# harness and 53/1 on the live tree. The one that flipped:
#     "a model-runtime environment still gets every marker"
# Because BOTH consumers live inside the bridge process tree -- the model's own
# CLI is spawned by the bridge too -- the ancestor rule outranked the model
# marker for the model as well. Measured on the live tree with that patch on:
#     resolve_surface()                      -> owner
#     resolve_surface(env=+CLAUDECODE=1)     -> owner    <- the model branch
#     resolve_surface(..., ancestors=[])     -> machine     was dead code
# So D3 would have regressed silently in production while the branch that was
# supposed to protect it passed in every test.
#
# THE RULE THAT CAME OUT OF IT: when ambient classification (ancestors, env
# sniffing, process shape) overrides explicit marking, that branch dies in
# production and lives in tests. Two consumers inside the same process tree
# cannot be told apart by anything ambient -- any such predicate is a coin flip
# that happens to land right in a harness. So there is no predicate here. The
# surface is a DECLARED argument at the call site, and resolve_surface() below
# reads nothing but that declaration.
#
# WHICH WAY THE DEFAULT FAILS. The two failure modes are not symmetric:
#     default owner + a caller forgets to declare  -> a MACHINE reader loses a
#         signal. Degraded, and loud: D3's own discipline ("whether a ramp was
#         produced is a value; silence is not an answer") makes an absent
#         RAMP_DECISION something the reader is already trained to notice.
#     default machine + a new rail opens          -> the OWNER reads machine
#         text on the owner's phone. That is the live defect, reproduced silently, on
#         the day someone opens a rail without knowing this file exists.
# Fail toward the benign one: DEFAULT_SURFACE is the owner surface.
#
# THE SAME FIX ALREADY EXISTS TWICE IN THIS PACK, both times at the consumer:
#   exercise-enter._CARD_MACHINE_MARKERS  "machine markers must NEVER reach the
#     pin ... Strip bare machine markers on the way to the pin only; stdout
#     keeps them."
#   session-closeout._card_body           trims set-table's own telemetry tail
#     before the close-out card.
# Both had to KNOW about set-table's tail. This is that pattern moved to
# set-table's own boundary, with the consumer naming its audience instead of
# re-deriving the token list.
#
# WHY THE DISTINCTION IS IN-BAND, NOT A SECOND STREAM. Routing signals to
# stderr (or an fd) looks cleaner and is defeated by one caller writing `2>&1`,
# which splices them straight back into the message body. The decision is
# carried by the declared audience and applied to the TEXT on stdout -- never
# by which fd a line went out on. For the same reason a dropped line is dropped
# outright and is NOT mirrored to stderr.
#
# THREE actions, because suppression and narration are different sets:
#   drop   the whole line is machine-only; the owner gains nothing from it
#   strip  the `TOKEN: ` prefix is machine, the PAYLOAD is prose they need
#   pass   untouched -- everything not named below
#
# MATCHED BY KNOWN TOKEN, NEVER BY SHAPE. A `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{2,}` rule eats
# `RIR 2로 맞추세요` -- RIR is core workout vocabulary -- and `API 문서를
# 확인해주세요`. Both are legitimate owner prose on this domain. (The same rule
# the failed ancestor probe broke, one layer up: match the thing, not a shape
# that resembles it.)
#
# ONE TABLE, ON PURPOSE. The framework lane is designing an in-band sigil
# scheme to replace token lists at the bridge layer; its format is not locked,
# so nothing here is built against it. When it lands, this tuple is the only
# thing that changes and no call site moves.
SIGNAL_POLICY = (
    # token,            form,    action
    ("DAY_SOURCE",       "kv",    "drop"),
    ("SESSION_UNTAGGED", "kv",    "drop"),
    ("RAMP_DECISION",    "kv",    "drop"),
    ("PREP_PLANNED",     "kv",    "drop"),
    ("WT_UNKNOWN",       "kv",    "drop"),
    # 2026-09-01 22:35 -- MAT_PREP_HOST 는 매트프렙 주인 결정의 감사 라인이다.
